# screen — real-world examples

GNU screen: keep terminal sessions alive across disconnects.

## Start a named session

```sh
screen -S session_name
```

## Detach / re-attach

```sh
# Detach from the current session:  Ctrl-a then d

# Re-attach a detached session
screen -r session_name

# Force-detach elsewhere and attach here (after a dropped connection)
screen -dr session_name
```

## List sessions

```sh
screen -ls
```

## Homelab examples

```sh
# Kick off a long zypper dup over ssh and survive a disconnect
ssh david@homelab
screen -S dup
sudo zypper dup        # then Ctrl-a d to detach, log off safely
# later:
screen -r dup

# Run a long media re-encode you can check on later
screen -S encode
handbrakecli -i in.mkv -o out.mp4 --preset "Fast 1080p30"

# Reattach from anywhere; if it says "Attached", steal it:
screen -dr encode
```

## Key bindings (prefix is Ctrl-a)

| Keys | Action |
|------|--------|
| `Ctrl-a d` | Detach |
| `Ctrl-a c` | New window |
| `Ctrl-a n` / `Ctrl-a p` | Next / previous window |
| `Ctrl-a "` | List windows to pick |
| `Ctrl-a A` | Rename current window |
| `Ctrl-a k` | Kill current window |
| `Ctrl-a [` | Enter scrollback/copy mode |

## CLI flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-S <name>` | Start named session |
| `-r <name>` | Reattach |
| `-d -r` | Detach elsewhere, then reattach |
| `-ls` | List sessions |
| `-x` | Attach to an already-attached session (multi-view) |
